<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mariel boatlift</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mariel boatlift was the mass emigration of about 125,000 Cubans from the port of Mariel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> between April and October 1980. It began after thousands of Cubans sought asylum at the Peruvian embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidel-castro">Fidel Castro</a> allowed those who wished to leave to go, with exiles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> sending boats. The Cuban government also put prisoners and psychiatric patients aboard, causing controversy in the United States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mariel-boatlift.jpg" alt="Mariel boatlift" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Department of Homeland Security · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariel_Refugees.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The exodus was set off by a sharp downturn in the Cuban economy and followed decades of Cuban emigration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In the late 1970s President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a> had tried to improve relations, lifting travel restrictions, and in September 1977 both countries opened an Interests Section in each other's capital, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>'s support for Soviet interventions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> kept relations strained. Cubans had already sought asylum in foreign embassies: in 1979 a group crashed a bus into the Venezuelan embassy, and early in 1980 the Peruvian and Venezuelan embassies took in asylum seekers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After some 10,000 Cubans crowded into the grounds of the Peruvian embassy, the government let them leave. About 7,500 first went by air, at first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>. On 20 April Fidel Castro declared the port of Mariel open to anyone with someone to collect them, and Cuban Americans began sending boats; the first reached Key West on 21 April with 48 refugees, and by 25 April as many as 300 boats were in the harbour. Around 1,700 boats made the crossing. The Cuban government treated the exodus as a cleansing of undesirables and organised acts of repudiation against those leaving.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the refugees were processed in camps around Miami, including the Orange Bowl, then moved to bases such as Fort Chaffee, where riots broke out. Carter's administration classed them with Haitian arrivals as Cuban-Haitian entrants. The two governments ended the boatlift by agreement in late October 1980.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Most of the refugees were ordinary Cubans, tens of thousands of them Seventh-Day Adventists or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehovahs-witnesses">Jehovah's Witnesses</a>; 2,746, or 2.2 percent, were classed as serious or violent criminals and denied citizenship. They gained permanent legal status in 1984 under a revision of the Cuban Adjustment Act. David Card's study of Miami found that the arrivals, about half of whom settled there, had only a marginal effect on wages. Carter's opponents called the boatlift a failure of his administration, a CBS and New York Times poll of June 1980 found 71% of Americans disapproving, and in November 1980 voters in Miami Dade County removed Spanish as a second official language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mariel boatlift” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state provided free health care and education while suppressing dissent, and over a million Cubans emigrated, many in the <b>Mariel boatlift</b> of 1980.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cuba">Republic of Cuba</a></li></ul></section>
